  "worked_solution": "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-6}{2-(-6)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{4}$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{4}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+b \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{4}(2)+b \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{2}+b \\\\ 0+\\frac{3}{2} & =b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the equation of the line is $y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\,x+\\frac{3}{2}.$",
